Date: Mon, 04 Oct 93 06:11:44 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: EVERETT INTERPRETATION

John K Clark, talking about the Everett many-worlds metatheory of QM:

> In 1957 Hugh Everett ... said that when any particle undergoes
> the smallest detectable change ( a quantum jump ) the entire
> universe duplicates itself and splits in two

The splitting process can be interpreted as localised within the light
cone of an event.  Everett talked about the observer being split, along
the line of the recent "many-minds" interpretation, rather than the
whole universe all at once, as many-worlds is often falsely represented
as implying.  One reason why I prefer the term "many-worlds" over "many-
universes" is that "world" has more parochial connotations than
"universe".  This language is less suggestive of the (false) notion that
experiments in the Andromeda galaxy are splitting us now, and vice versa. 
Rather each quantum interaction causes local splitting that spreads out
across the universe.

> If true this would mean an infinite number of universes.

In fact the number of worlds, W, is large but finite, scaling
exponentially with the entropy released, according to the
Boltzmann-Planck law (entropy = k log (W)).  Since k is very small (10^-
34 J/K) then a typical irreversible macroscopic process generates
something more than 10^(10^30) parallel worlds.  Very large, admittedly,
but not infinite.

There was a discussion about QM, falsifiablity, retrodiction vs
prediction etc a couple of months ago, I think.  If anyone has relevant
posts from these legendary archives they might like to repost some now.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

PS nice to agree with James Donald for once :-)

PPS the implications of many-worlds for cryonics are left as an exercise
for the reader.

