Date: Mon, 04 Oct 93 10:28:35 -0400
From: Tim Freeman <tsf@cs.cmu.edu>
Subject: DIET: Too little fat causes brain damage! 

Robert Brooks <rb@hprrb.rose.hp.com> quotes Dr. Whelan Dowd of Harvard
University School of Medicine as saying:

>"My theory to explain all this is that fat is actually a nutrient for the
>brain.  It's well known that the brain is over 90% fat.  So it's not all
>that surprising that when the body's fat level drops to a certain point, the
>brain literally starts to get digested, just as muscle gets digested in
>people suffering from starvation."

The analogy is only valid if the body cannot synthesize fats from
a low-fat diet just as it cannot synthesize some proteins.

There is another plausible interpretation: perhaps fat-soluble
nutrients cannot be absorbed when there is too little fat in the diet.
Experiments could distinguish the possibilities; in one case we would
expect blood levels of certain vitamins to be low, and in the other we
would expect the amount of fat in the brain as estimated from, say, an
MRI scan would be decreasing (I'm only guessing that MRI scans can
yield enough information to compute this).  In [Weindruch88] or maybe
[Walford86] I think it says that <10% calories from fat shortens the
life time of lab rats; he was guessing that vitamin absorption was the
problem.

I don't know offhand if there is a fat-soluble vitamin where a
deficiency causes brain damage, but some back issue of Cryonics
pointed to experiments with cats saying that vitamin E protects
somewhat against free-radical damage to the brain from ischemia, and
vitamin E is indeed fat-soluble.

By the way, may I forward your article to the fatfree mailing list?
(Reply privately, not to extropians.)  It would be interesting to
observe their response.  If you want me to, I'll take your name off so
you will be spared from observing their response.  :-)

Tim

