Harry S. Hawk writes:

>I would perfer ethically over morally, as I see morals as having
>a religious foundation while I see ethics as having a "rational"
>foundation.

Hmm. The way that I understand the terms, a *moral* is a personally
held principle concerning the "rightness" or "goodness" of a behavior.
An *ethic* is a moral principle that is held to be true within a
particular social framework. Either can be justified from intellectual,
religious, or other points of view.

Individuals have morals, social groups have ethics. Groups of people
with different moral principles can create a single ethical system that
encompasses them all (its not that easy, usually, but as an anarchist,
I certainly believe that it's possible). And conversely, an individual
with a well-defined set of moral principles can function effectively in
social environments with different ethical systems (depending on the
robustness of one's moral principles - a good reason to base them on a
combination of rationality and a good knowledge of human nature).

If one works with the terms as defined thus, then the EI info blurb
makes sense.

