Date: Mon, 04 Oct 93 15:18:10 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: free will: Part Three -- The ethics

Part Three -- The ethics of free-will

The utility of a belief system depends upon its truthfulness and
credibility, its effect on the subjective mental states of its
proponents (does it raise or lower their happiness overall?), and its
effect on the behaviour of its adherents (does it condemn them to
death by burial or cremation within a few decades for instance, and
does it motivate them to treat each other well?).

Where a belief is so patently false that it can readily be falsified
or disproved, eg white elephants fly over your backyard and land every
eighty-two minutes, it is useless to all sane people.  But often we find
there are several alternative explanations, or 'interpretations', for
some particular phenomena, which all have a truth value of greater than
zero, all roughly the same.  In such cases consideration has to be given
to philosophical satisfaction and effect on behaviour.  Of course,
Occam's Razor can be applied where all else is equal, but whether all
else is equal can easily be a matter of personal taste.   

Let's assume that no-one actually has free-will.  At least, we'll assume
that everyone believes this.  From the individual's point of view, this
seems rather deleterious.  It's well-known (from experiments on rats for
example) that stress is due more to a feeling of not being in control
and not being able to avoid so-called stressful events, than the actual
events themselves.  A minor advantage is that past mistakes can be
rationalised away -- "I couldn't help it" -- but there would then be
less motivation to avoid mistakes in the future, or to engage in
beneficial action.   

I think everyone would prefer to believe that they personally 'really'
have free-will.  And to pretend that something is so when you really
believe otherwise seems impractical.  There would be advantages in
believing that it is only everyone else that are robots -- "He did me
wrong, but he can't help it, he's an imbecile" -- but the real problems
come when the lawmakers or protection agencies believe no one can control
themselves.  

If it is deemed that people aren't responsible for their own actions, 
this seems like an argument for doing away with punishment and
encouraging crime.  If the penalty for crime is too low, this benefits
the bad at the expense of the good, who are more likely to become
victims.  (A bit like victim disarmament).  True, it's ridiculous to have
crazy metaphysical arguments by lawyers such as, "At the time of the
alleged offense, the accused was not in full control of his own mind."
If someone is capable of robbing a little old lady in her own home and
breaking her arm or jaw for kicks, or robbing a boy of his bicycle and
busting his nose, then they're capable of being made to feel pain, losing
their liberty, and working to make partial restitution to the victim.
Being "mentally disturbed" should be no excuse.  They're all guilty, I
say!  

Of course, it's wrong when people are punished for victimless 'crimes',
viewed by governments as "crimes against the state".  Most sadism is
carried out by agents of governments that abuse their powers (or obey
orders), particularly in totalitarian regimes.  But I don't think 
abolition of belief in free-will would reduce the amount of sadism -- 
it would probably increase the level of crime. 
 
Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

