From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: BLACKNET: The truth is thought provoking

Perry takes me to task for suggesting that Chaumian digicash
will be less private than current cash (and I hope he's right
but I have some concerns):

> Not at all! Look at your dollar bills in your pocket. Note that they
> each have a serial number. Nothing to stop you from recording the
> numbers on the bills you hand someone.

True, but it is not (yet, I think) required by law and is certainly
more inconvenient than automatic record-keeping of digital money.  The
point I'm trying to make is that it's quite unlikely that the IRS and
Congress (which requires the IRS for its spending money) will simply
roll over.  "Digicash" is not a monolithic privacy-preserving mechansim;
there are lots of variations.  The important thing is what actually ends
up being implemented.

Perhaps we can have a Conversation on this topic.  I base my initial
concerns (mostly unrelated to the above political/legal influences on
FutureSpace) on two things:

* In Chaum's 1985 CACM article, he states (p. 1036): "Lack of auditability
also allows paper currency to be conveniently used for illicit payments
such as bribes, extortion, and black-market purchases.... These problems
are solved with the new systems..."  Now, since black-market purchases
are quite feasible with cash as it currently exists (not that I personally
have ever made an illegal payment, of course), isn't it true that the
claimed deterrence of black-market transactions means Chaum's digicash
is less private than current cash?

* A technical issue that interests me is _transferability_ of digital
coins without any involvement or eventual knowledge of the banks.  I
can receive cash and spend it again without any bank anywhere knowing
about it.  If I understand Chaum's digicash protocol (and I hope I
don't), it is necessary for the bank to be informed of a payment if
the recipient wants to make sure that I don't spend a "copy" of the
money quickly elsewhere.  Although the coin can't be traced back to
me, the necessity for the bank's involvement in the transfer makes
the system less private than regular cash.

derek


