Date: Mon, 4 Oct 93 09:51:15 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com (Hal Finney)
Subject: CRYPTO: Problems with crypto-anarchy

Please note the subject change.  This is a followup to the Blacknet thread.

I remember now that the Extortion Explosion post was by some Greek girl
named Cassandra.  I think she replied to Perry's argument that Chaumian
cash could be traced by the payor+bank by asking whether all cash systems
would have that property.

I think Derek is right that not all forms of digicash will necessarily
allow for the kinds of tracing that would allow illegal cash payees to
be revealed by the payor collaborating with the bank.  Presumably in a
free market different forms of cash may coexist, and in such a
situation extorters would demand payment in the forms whose anonymity
could not be broken.

I would feel like a hypocrite if I advocated free cryptography with the
attendant anonymity, but "drew the line" at truly anonymous digital cash,
calling instead for restrictions on cash implementations to ensure that
cash could be traced given sufficient social need.  Shades of Clipper!

I don't have my CP archives handy, but I do think there were some other
arguments raised against Cassandra's prophecies.  One was that this same
ease of anonymous payments might make it easier for police to maintain
their own network of anonymous informants, because there would no longer
be any need for physical meetings between cops and stoolies.  Similarly,
rewards could be offered for evidence leading to criminal conviction, again
while maintaining the anonymity of the whistle blower.

Maybe you could even think about allowing anonymous testimony in a court
trial, although obviously that flies in the face of the common-law notion
that an accused should be able to face his accuser.  But perhaps digital
reputation systems could allow a judge or jury to evaluate the credibility
of someone's testimony even without knowing his identity.

I think the important thing to keep in mind is that these kinds of changes
ARE GOING TO HAPPEN unless the government crackdown on crypto succeeds.
We all deserve privacy; in that sense, everyone has something to hide.
But criminals, by and large, have more to hide than other people.  Adding
crypto to the mix will therefore shift the balance of power away from
law-abiders and towards law-breakers.

How you respond to this prospect will depend to some extent on how you
view the law.  But people should go into this with their eyes open.

Hal
