From: xiaozhou@phoenix.Princeton.EDU (Kate Xiao Zhou)
Subject: free will

At 10:02 PM 10/2/93 -0700, John K Clark wrote:
[quoting out of context]
> no point in making  a man suffer for an act you don't like, he couldn't help
>it 

Does this follow from the anti-free will position? If so, the free will
question boils down to an empirical question - do criminals respond to
incentives such as the severity and likelihood of punishment? My impression
is that they do.

But I don't think that is the same as saying they have free will, since
evolution can make species of bacteria respond to 'incentives.' That's not
what people have in mind when they argue about this stuff, as far as I can
tell. Hence my question about practical differences between free will and
its opposite. How could I tell whether a sample of bacteria has free will?
What would I do differently if I thought I didn't?

